    "minimum_years_to_graduate": 5,
    "maximum_years_to_graduate": 8,
    "repeat_attempts_rule": "maximum_years_to_graduate - course_year",
    "failing_grades": ["F"],
    "_comment": "A course is repeated only after it is failed. Once it is passed it cannot be taken again, so a sequence of sittings always ends at the first pass. Within that rule, a course first taken in year x may be repeated in each remaining year up to the maximum of 8, giving (8 - x) repeat attempts and (9 - x) sittings in total: 8 for a First Year course, 4 for a Fifth Year course. Every sitting counts separately in the GPA - its units enter the denominator again and its points enter the numerator again - so repeating a course degrades the GPA in the same way that taking an extra course would.",
    "_failing_grades_note": "Confirmed with the department on 2026-09-17: E is a pass, and only F mandates a re-sit. A course graded E is therefore closed and cannot be taken again. Should this ever change, adding a grade to failing_grades is the only edit needed."
  },
  "reporting": {
    "gpa_decimal_places": 2,
    "gpa_full_precision_places": 5,
    "_comment": "gpa_decimal_places is the everyday presentation (2 is what the university reports). gpa_full_precision_places is what the 'Show full precision' switch in the interface reveals. Both are display settings only - the GPA is always computed at full precision from integer points and integer units, so more places expose digits that were already there rather than improving accuracy. The degree classification always uses the gpa_decimal_places figure, so the switch can never move a student between classes."
  },
